Embedded subprocesses (optional `subProcesses` list): set when the source describes a group of steps handled together as a single composite step — cues: "the X process, which involves …", "as part of handling Y: first a, then b, then c", "the X sub-flow / block / stage", a named bundle of sub-steps treated as one unit. Leave empty when the process is flat.
- Each entry has `id`, `name`, and `memberIds` — the ids of its member elements. A subprocess can group decisions, intermediate throws, and end states alongside activities. Those members stay listed in their respective top-level arrays (`activities`, `decisions`, etc.); the subprocess only NAMES which of them it groups.
- An element belongs to at most one subprocess. List at least one member per subprocess.
- Members are interior steps: the main flow enters and leaves the subprocess through its own `id`, never through a member's id directly. A `flows` edge must not name a member as its `from` or `to` unless the other endpoint is also a member of the same subprocess.
- The interior's ends are implicit — do NOT draw an edge from a member to the subprocess's own `id` to mark the group finishing, nor from the subprocess's own `id` to a member to mark it starting. The interior starts at whichever member has no incoming edge from another member, and finishes at whichever member has no outgoing edge to another member. Where the interior branches and several of those branches finish, each just stops; they are NOT joined back to the subprocess's `id`. Membership alone says where the group ends, so an edge naming both a member and its own subprocess is always wrong and must be deleted rather than redirected.
- A branch of a decision inside a subprocess is the exception to "just stops": every branch must be realised by a flow, so it cannot be left dangling. When a branch means the group is finished, add an `endStates` entry, list its id in the subprocess's `memberIds`, and point the branch at that — an end state can be a member like anything else. Never point such a branch at the subprocess's own `id`.
- Reserve a subprocess for a genuine grouping the source calls out; a plain linear sequence of ordinary steps stays ungrouped.
- Example: `{id: "sub-assess", name: "Assess claim", memberIds: ["act-validate", "dec-estimate", "act-decide"]}`.
